  "body": "Fixes #337937\n\n### Summary\nWhen extensions contribute to environment variables (such as `PATH`) using `EnvironmentVariableMutatorType.Append` or `Prepend`, the existing environment variable value is concatenated directly via string addition (`+`).\n\nIf the existing environment variable happens to have a trailing carriage return (`\\r`) or newline (`\\n`)—which can happen via manual environment variable configuration, batch files, or external tools/installers—appending an extension path produces a string with an embedded `\\r`. \n\nOn Windows integrated terminals:\n1. Printing `$env:Path` causes the cursor to return to the beginning of the line upon hitting `\\r`, overwriting earlier characters with the appended path and visually truncating the line.\n2. The path entry containing the trailing `\\r` becomes an invalid filesystem path on Windows, breaking command resolution.\n\n### Proposed Changes\n- In `MergedEnvironmentVariableCollection.applyToProcessEnvironment`:\n  - `EnvironmentVariableMutatorType.Append`: Trim trailing `[\\r\\n]+$` from `env[actualVariable]` before appending.\n  - `EnvironmentVariableMutatorType.Prepend`: Trim leading `^[\\r\\n]+` from `env[actualVariable]` before prepending.\n- Restricted the sanitization strictly to `[\\r\\n]` so that full-width Unicode characters (e.g., Japanese directory paths with full-width spaces `\\u3000`) and standard spaces/symbols remain completely intact.\n- Added comprehensive unit test cases to `environmentVariableCollection.test.ts` covering CR/LF sanitization and full-width multi-byte paths.\n\n### Verification Done\n- Verified with unit tests covering trailing `\\r`, `\\r\\n`, `\\n`, multiple newlines, and Japanese characters with ideographic spaces.\n- Verified that trailing newlines are safely stripped while paths and full-width spaces remain intact.\n",
  "rebaseable": true
 },
